Hey everyone,

I hope you're doing well. I wanted to share a quick update regarding the issue at hand.

I've managed to find a workaround that might be helpful for those who are still seeking a solution while waiting for the merge requests with fixes to be merged. Although it's not a permanent fix, it could potentially provide some relief to those facing the problem in the meantime.

I've created an Angular directive called AriaLabelDirective that allows for the dynamic setting of aria-label attributes with support for interpolation and i18n. Here's how it works:

Define the directive:
```
@Directive({
  selector: '[appAriaLabel]',
})
export class AriaLabelDirective implements AfterViewInit {
  @Input()
  appAriaLabel?: string;

  constructor(private el: ElementRef, private renderer: Renderer2) {}

  ngAfterViewInit(): void {
    if (this.appAriaLabel) {
      this.renderer.setAttribute(this.el.nativeElement, 'aria-label', this.appAriaLabel);
    }
  }
}
```
Usage example with interpolation and i18n:
```
<div appAriaLabel="Title: {{title}}" i18n-appAriaLabel>
  ...
</div>
```
This directive allows you to set the aria-label attribute by providing the appAriaLabel input with an interpolated value that will be set dynamically in runtime, but still, i18n will take that translation in the compilation stage. Also, if you need to support translation for other attributes like 'title', you can simply extend the directive with additional @Input() properties and provide them on input in the needed html element.

For more complex cases where simple interpolation is not sufficient, such as when the text depends on a boolean variable, it's recommended to form your message in the .ts file using the $localize keyword to provide translation for static texts. Here's an example:

```
<div [attr.aria-label]="divAriaLabel">
  ...
</div>
```

```
const isTitle = true;
this.divAriaLabel = isTitle ? $localize`Title: ` : $localize`Caption: `;
this.divAriaLabel += this.title;
```
By using this approach, you can handle more complex scenarios and still benefit from the translation capabilities of the i18n package.

Please feel free to check out the workaround and share your thoughts or experiences. If it works for you or if you have any additional suggestions, it would be awesome to hear about it.

Let's keep the conversation going and help each other out until the official fixes are in place.
